OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026", "title": "The weak pinned planar distance theorem", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "167", "family_title": "Planar distinct distances and unit-distance bounds", "abstract_html": "We prove the weak pinned Erdős distinct-distance conjecture. For every fixed <i>ε</i> &amp;gt; 0, all but \\(o(n)\\) points of any <i>n</i>-point planar set determine at least \\(n^{1-\\varepsilon}\\) distinct nonzero distances.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(s>0\\), \\( F_n(s)\\longrightarrow0\\qquad(n\\longrightarrow\\infty). \\) No separation, general-position, or coordinate hypothesis is imposed.", "statement": "<p>Definitions:\nFor a finite set \\(P\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) and distinct \\(x,y\\in P\\), define the distance fiber size\n\\[\nk_P(x,y)=\n\\left|\\left\\{z\\in P\\setminus\\{x\\}:\n\\|z-x\\|_2=\\|y-x\\|_2\\right\\}\\right|.\n\\]\nThis counts \\(y\\) itself. For every integer \\(n\\ge2\\) and real \\(s&gt;0\\), put\n\\[\nF_n(s)=\n\\sup_{\\substack{P\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\\\ |P|=n}}\n\\frac{\\left|\\{(x,y)\\in P^2:x\\ne y,\\ k_P(x,y)\\ge n^s\\}\\right|}\n{n(n-1)}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(s&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nF_n(s)\\longrightarrow0\\qquad(n\\longrightarrow\\infty).\n\\]\nNo separation, general-position, or coordinate hypothesis is imposed. The convergence is uniform over all configurations; no explicit rate of decay is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose the theorem fails. Proposition 1 constructs the algebraic graph sequence with its fixed positive density, large fibers, marginal bounds, and uniform subset estimate. Proposition 2 constructs the finite nonnegative scales \\(W_2,A,W\\) and the exact additive overlap identity. Propositions 3 and 4 establish the small-cell and variance estimates.</p><p>Every sequence of nonnegative real numbers has a bounded subsequence or a subsequence tending to infinity. Proposition 5 excludes the latter possibility for \\(W\\), and Proposition 6 excludes the former. Hence the graph sequence cannot exist.</p><p>The assumption that \\(\\Theta(s)&gt;0\\) somewhere was false. Therefore\n\\[\nF_n(s)\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nfor every fixed \\(s&gt;0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 1.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), \\( \\sup_{\\substack{P\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\\\ |P|=n}} \\frac{\\left|\\{x\\in P:|D_x(P)|<n^{1-\\varepsilon}\\}\\right|}{n} \\longrightarrow0. \\) In particular, every sufficiently large \\(n\\)-point …", "statement": "<p>Definitions:\nFor a finite set \\(P\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) and \\(x\\in P\\), write\n\\[\nD_x(P)=\\{\\|y-x\\|_2:y\\in P\\setminus\\{x\\}\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\substack{P\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\\\ |P|=n}}\n\\frac{\\left|\\{x\\in P:|D_x(P)|&lt;n^{1-\\varepsilon}\\}\\right|}{n}\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nIn particular, every sufficiently large \\(n\\)-point planar set has a pin determining at least \\(n^{1-\\varepsilon}\\) distinct nonzero distances.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(\\varepsilon\\ge1\\), every pin in a set of at least two points has at least one distance.</p><p>Suppose \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;1\\), and take \\(s=\\varepsilon/2\\). At a pin with fewer than \\(n^{1-\\varepsilon}\\) distance classes, fewer than\n\\[\nn^{1-\\varepsilon+s}=n^{1-\\varepsilon/2}\n\\]\nneighbors lie in classes of size less than \\(n^s\\). Each such pin therefore contributes at least\n\\[\nn-1-n^{1-\\varepsilon/2}\n\\]\npairs counted by \\(F_n(s)\\).</p><p>If there are \\(b\\) such pins, then for sufficiently large \\(n\\),\n\\[\n\\frac bn\n\\le\n\\frac{(n-1)F_n(\\varepsilon/2)}\n{n-1-n^{1-\\varepsilon/2}}\n\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nby Theorem 1. The bound is independent of \\(P\\), proving the uniform assertion and its stated existence consequence.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Proposition 2.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a sequence of finite sets \\(P\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) of real algebraic points, with \\(n=|P|\\to\\infty\\), and nonempty directed graphs \\(E\\) on \\(P\\), such that …", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and fixed parameters:\nSuppose the conclusion of Theorem 1 fails. Using \\(F_n\\) from Theorem 1, put\n\\[\n\\Theta(t)=\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}F_n(t),\n\\qquad\ns_*=\\sup\\{t&gt;0:\\Theta(t)&gt;0\\}.\n\\]\nThen \\(0&lt;s_*\\le1\\). Fix\n\\[\n0&lt;c&lt;s_*/3,\\qquad\n\\max\\{c,(1-c)s_*\\}&lt;s&lt;s_*,\n\\qquad c&lt;\\gamma&lt;s,\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nwith \\(s\\) a continuity point of \\(\\Theta\\), and set\n\\[\n\\theta=\\Theta(s)&gt;0.\n\\]\nThese parameters remain fixed.</p><p>Definitions for a directed graph:\nFor a nonempty graph\n\\[\nE\\subset\\{(x,y)\\in P^2:x\\ne y\\}\n\\]\non an \\(n\\)-point set \\(P\\), partition the edges into fibers according to their source and squared distance. For a fiber \\(e\\), let \\(x_e\\) be its source, \\(F_e\\subset P\\setminus\\{x_e\\}\\) its target set, and \\(k_e=|F_e|\\). Let \\(d^-(y)\\) be the incoming degree of \\(y\\). Define:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha&amp;=\\text{uniform probability on }P,\\\\\n\\pi&amp;=\\text{uniform probability on the }n(n-1)\\text{ distinct ordered pairs},\\\\\nq&amp;=\\text{uniform probability on }E,\\quad\\text{with marginals }q_X,q_Y,\\\\\n\\mu_e&amp;=\\text{uniform probability on }F_e,\\\\\n\\rho_y&amp;=\\text{uniform probability on the incoming neighbors of }y\n\\quad(d^-(y)&gt;0).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\mathbb E_e\\) for expectation over fibers with weights \\(k_e/|E|\\). Thus sampling \\(e\\) with these weights and then \\(y\\sim\\mu_e\\), with \\(x=x_e\\), samples \\(q\\). Equivalently, sample \\(y\\sim q_Y\\) and then \\(x\\sim\\rho_y\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a sequence of finite sets \\(P\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) of real algebraic points, with \\(n=|P|\\to\\infty\\), and nonempty directed graphs \\(E\\) on \\(P\\), such that\n\\[\nd_n:=\\frac{|E|}{n(n-1)}\\longrightarrow\\theta,\n\\qquad\nk_e\\ge n^{s-o(1)}\n\\]\nuniformly in \\(e\\). For a fixed constant \\(B_0\\),\n\\[\nq\\le B_0\\pi,\\qquad\nq_X,q_Y\\le B_0\\alpha,\\qquad\n\\frac{q_X+q_Y}{2}\\ge(1-o(1))\\alpha\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\npointwise, and\n\\[\nq_Y\\{y:d^-(y)&lt;n^\\gamma\\}\n=O(n^{\\gamma-1})=o(1).\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\nFinally, putting \\(\\lambda_n=1/\\log n\\), for every \\(C\\subset P\\), with \\(m=|C|\\) and \\(p_e=\\mu_e(C)\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_e\\mathbf1_C(x_e)p_e\\mathbf1_{\\{p_e\\ge\\lambda_n\\}}\n\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n(1+o(1))\\pi(C^2),&amp;m&gt;n^{1-c},\\\\\no(1)\\pi(C^2),&amp;m\\le n^{1-c}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\nAll errors are uniform in vertices, fibers, and subsets wherever these occur. All asymptotic constants may depend on the fixed parameters, but not on configurations or coordinates.</p>", "proof": "<p>Each \\(F_n\\), and hence \\(\\Theta\\), is nonincreasing. Moreover, \\(F_n(t)=0\\) for \\(t\\ge1\\), since a distance class has at most \\(n-1\\) points. Failure of Theorem 1 therefore gives \\(0&lt;s_*\\le1\\). The interval for choosing \\(s\\) in (2.1) is nonempty, and a monotone function has at most countably many discontinuities. Since \\(s&lt;s_*\\), monotonicity gives \\(\\theta&gt;0\\).</p><p>Preserving the exact distance pattern:\nFor fixed \\(N\\), the numerator defining \\(F_N(s)\\) takes values in the finite set\n\\[\n\\{0,1,\\ldots,N(N-1)\\}.\n\\]\nIts maximum is attained, without a compactness assertion about configurations. Choose a sequence \\(N\\to\\infty\\) on which \\(F_N(s)\\to\\theta\\), and an attaining configuration for each \\(N\\).</p><p>Each configuration can be replaced by one with real algebraic coordinates without changing any distance class. Label its points by \\(1,\\ldots,N\\), use coordinate variables \\((u_i,b_i)\\), and write\n\\[\nD_{ij}=(u_i-u_j)^2+(b_i-b_j)^2.\n\\]\nRequire \\(D_{ij}&gt;0\\) for \\(i&lt;j\\), and prescribe, for every two unordered pairs, the equality \\(D_{ij}=D_{kl}\\) or disequality \\(D_{ij}\\ne D_{kl}\\) holding in the original configuration. These are finitely many polynomial conditions over \\(\\mathbb Q\\); a disequality can be expressed as\n\\[\n(D_{ij}-D_{kl})^2&gt;0.\n\\]\nTheir existential sentence holds over \\(\\mathbb R\\), and therefore over the real algebraic numbers by transfer for real closed fields. The resulting points are distinct and retain the exact distance equality pattern. Thus every class size, and the attaining value of \\(F_N(s)\\), is unchanged. The real threshold \\(N^s\\) need not enter the transferred system: its comparison with each preserved integer class size is unchanged. No degree or height bound is imposed.</p><p>Deleting vertices and small fibers:\nInitially keep every edge counted by \\(F_N(s)\\), so every initial fiber has at least \\(N^s\\) edges. Define\n\\[\nK_N=\\frac{N^s}{\\log N},\\qquad\nf_N=\\frac{\\log K_N}{\\log N}\n=s-\\frac{\\log\\log N}{\\log N},\\qquad\nm_0=\\lceil N/2\\rceil.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(N\\), \\(f_N&gt;0\\). Lemma 1.1 gives\n\\[\nb_N:=\\max_{m_0\\le m\\le N}\\bigl(F_m(f_N)-\\theta\\bigr)_+\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nt_N=b_N+|F_N(s)-\\theta|+\\frac1{\\log N},\n\\qquad\n\\eta_N=\\sqrt{t_N}.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\eta_N\\to0\\), and eventually \\(\\theta-\\eta_N&gt;0\\).</p><p>Whenever a remaining nonempty fiber has fewer than \\(K_N\\) edges, discard it. If the current vertex count is \\(m\\), and a vertex has the sum of its outgoing and incoming degrees less than\n\\[\n2(\\theta-\\eta_N)(m-1),\n\\]\ndelete that vertex and its incident edges. Clean out small fibers after each vertex deletion, and continue until all degree lower bounds hold.</p><p>There are at most \\(N(N-1)/N^s\\) initial fibers. Restricting vertices never splits a source-distance fiber, and each initial fiber can be discarded only once, at a cost below \\(K_N\\). All fiber cleanups together cost at most\n\\[\n\\frac{N(N-1)}{\\log N}\n\\]\nedges.</p><p>Suppose the process reaches \\(m_0\\) vertices. Put\n\\[\nA_N=N(N-1),\\qquad B_N=m_0(m_0-1).\n\\]\nThe total cost of vertex deletions is less than\n\\[\n2(\\theta-\\eta_N)\\sum_{m=m_0+1}^N(m-1)\n=(\\theta-\\eta_N)(A_N-B_N).\n\\]\nAfter cleanup, the number of remaining edges is at least\n\\[\nF_N(s)A_N-(\\theta-\\eta_N)(A_N-B_N)-\\frac{A_N}{\\log N}.\n\\]\nIts excess over \\((\\theta+b_N)B_N\\) is at least\n\\[\n\\eta_N(A_N-B_N)-t_NA_N\n=\n\\eta_NA_N\\left(1-\\frac{B_N}{A_N}-\\eta_N\\right)&gt;0\n\\]\nfor large \\(N\\), since \\(B_N/A_N\\to1/4\\). But every remaining edge belongs to a fiber of size at least\n\\[\nK_N=N^{f_N}\\ge m_0^{f_N},\n\\]\nso its density is at most \\(F_{m_0}(f_N)\\le\\theta+b_N\\), a contradiction.</p><p>The process stops on a set \\(P\\) of size \\(n&gt;m_0\\), with every nonempty fiber of size at least \\(K_N\\). Summing the degree lower bounds and using the definition of \\(b_N\\) gives\n\\[\n\\theta-\\eta_N\\le d_n\\le F_n(f_N)\\le\\theta+b_N.\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\nThe upper bound applies because \\(K_N\\ge n^{f_N}\\). Thus \\(d_n\\to\\theta\\) and \\(k_e\\ge n^{s-o(1)}\\) uniformly.</p><p>Probability bounds:\nFor every edge,\n\\[\n\\frac{q(x,y)}{\\pi(x,y)}=\\frac1{d_n},\n\\]\nso \\(B_0=2/\\theta\\) works for large \\(n\\). Summing over a coordinate gives the marginal upper bounds. At every vertex, the degree lower bound yields\n\\[\n\\frac{q_X(x)+q_Y(x)}2\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\theta-\\eta_N}{d_n}\\alpha(x)\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\theta-\\eta_N}{\\theta+b_N}\\alpha(x)\n=(1-o(1))\\alpha(x).\n\\]\nOnly the sum of the marginals is bounded below. Also,\n\\[\nq_Y\\{y:d^-(y)&lt;n^\\gamma\\}\n\\le\\frac{n\\,n^\\gamma}{|E|}\n=O(n^{\\gamma-1})=o(1),\n\\]\nbecause \\(\\gamma&lt;s&lt;1\\).</p><p>Large fibers inside subsets:\nSet\n\\[\nT_n=\\lambda_nK_N\n=\\frac{N^s}{\\log N\\log n}.\n\\]\nSince \\(N/2&lt;n\\le N\\), there is \\(\\delta_n\\to0\\) such that\n\\[\nT_n\\ge n^{s-\\delta_n};\n\\]\nin particular, \\(T_n\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>Multiplying the left side of (2.4) by \\(|E|\\) counts exactly the edges \\((x_e,y)\\in C^2\\) for which\n\\[\n|F_e\\cap C|\\ge\\lambda_nk_e.\n\\]\nEach counted pair satisfies\n\\[\nk_C(x_e,y)\\ge |F_e\\cap C|\\ge T_n.\n\\]\nA nonzero contribution requires \\(m-1\\ge T_n\\). Thus bounded subsets eventually contribute zero, and \\(m=0,1\\) always contribute zero.</p><p>If \\(m&gt;n^{1-c}\\), then\n\\[\nT_n\\ge n^{s-\\delta_n}\\ge m^{s-\\delta_n}.\n\\]\nLemma 1.1 bounds \\(F_m(s-\\delta_n)\\le\\theta+o(1)\\) uniformly over these sizes. Dividing the pair count by \\(|E|\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_e\\mathbf1_C(x_e)p_e\\mathbf1_{\\{p_e\\ge\\lambda_n\\}}\n\\le\n\\frac{\\theta+o(1)}{d_n}\\frac{m(m-1)}{n(n-1)}\n=(1+o(1))\\pi(C^2).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(m\\le n^{1-c}\\), choose a fixed\n\\[\ns_*&lt;s'&lt;\\frac{s}{1-c},\n\\]\npossible by (2.1). For large \\(n\\),\n\\[\nT_n\\ge n^{s-\\delta_n}\\ge n^{(1-c)s'}\\ge m^{s'}.\n\\]\nThe definition of \\(s_*\\) gives \\(\\Theta(s')=0\\), hence \\(F_m(s')\\to0\\) as \\(m\\to\\infty\\). Every contributing subset has \\(m\\ge T_n+1\\), so\n\\[\n\\sup_{m\\ge T_n+1}F_m(s')\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nDividing the pair bound by \\(|E|\\) gives \\(o(1)\\pi(C^2)\\) uniformly. This also covers \\(s'&gt;1\\), when the count is identically zero.</p><p>Henceforth, all configurations and graphs are from this sequence. In particular, \\(k_e\\ge n^\\gamma\\) for sufficiently large \\(n\\). Their sampling laws do not depend on any absolute values or grids introduced below.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume the definitions and fixed choice of \\(s,\\theta\\) in Proposition 1.", "statement": "<p>Assume the definitions and fixed choice of \\(s,\\theta\\) in Proposition 1. If \\(r_N\\to s\\) and \\(M_N\\to\\infty\\), then\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\substack{m\\ge M_N\\\\m\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ m\\ge2}}\n\\bigl(F_m(r_N)-\\theta\\bigr)_+\n\\longrightarrow0,\n\\]\nwhere \\((u)_+=\\max\\{u,0\\}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Given \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), continuity permits a fixed \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\Theta(s-\\delta)&lt;\\theta+\\epsilon/2.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(m\\),\n\\[\nF_m(s-\\delta)\\le\\Theta(s-\\delta)+\\epsilon/2\n\\]\nby the definition of limsup. For sufficiently large \\(N\\), one has \\(r_N\\ge s-\\delta\\), and all \\(m\\ge M_N\\) are in this range. Monotonicity bounds every \\(F_m(r_N)\\) by \\(\\theta+\\epsilon\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The quantities \\( S^*(x)=\\langle S_1(x)+S_2(x)\\rangle, \\qquad L^*(x,y)=\\langle L_1(x,y)+L_2(x,y)\\rangle \\quad(x\\ne y) \\) are finite, and for an absolute constant \\(\\kappa\\) …", "statement": "<p>Assumptions:\nFix one algebraic configuration \\(P\\) from Proposition 1, with \\(n=|P|\\ge2\\). Take a number field \\(K\\subset\\mathbb C\\) containing its coordinates and \\(i=\\sqrt{-1}\\). For \\(x=(u,b)\\), put\n\\[\nZ_1(x)=u+ib,\\qquad Z_2(x)=u-ib.\n\\]\nBoth maps are injective, and their differences remain nonzero under every embedding of \\(K\\). For distinct \\(x,y\\),\n\\[\nt(x,y):=\\|x-y\\|_2^2\n=(Z_1(y)-Z_1(x))(Z_2(y)-Z_2(x)).\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]</p><p>Places and raw depths:\nAt a finite place \\(v\\) above a prime \\(p\\), use the absolute value extending \\(|p|_p=p^{-1}\\), with\n\\[\n\\sigma_v=\\frac{[K_v:\\mathbb Q_p]}{[K:\\mathbb Q]}.\n\\]\nAll archimedean places are complex, since \\(i\\in K\\); there use ordinary complex modulus and\n\\[\n\\sigma_v=\\frac2{[K:\\mathbb Q]}.\n\\]\nThe normalized product formula is\n\\[\n\\sum_v\\sigma_v\\log|z|_v=0\\quad(z\\in K\\setminus\\{0\\}),\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{v\\mid\\infty}\\sigma_v=1.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nAt a place \\(v\\), with \\(v\\) suppressed in notation, define\n\\[\ni_j(x,y)=-\\log|Z_j(x)-Z_j(y)|_v\n\\quad(x\\ne y,\\ j=1,2).\n\\]\nOn a graph fiber \\(e\\), let \\(t_e\\) be its squared distance and put\n\\[\nh_e=-\\log|t_e|_v.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\ni_1(x_e,y)+i_2(x_e,y)=h_e\n\\quad(y\\in F_e).\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]</p><p>Hierarchies:\nAt finite places, set \\(d_j(x,y)=i_j(x,y)\\) for \\(x\\ne y\\).\nAt each complex place and for each \\(j\\), independently of vertex and fiber sampling, choose a uniformly random orientation and \\(\\xi\\) uniform on \\([0,\\log2)\\). Use nested square grids with sides\n\\[\nl_m=e^\\xi2^m,\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb Z.\n\\]\nChoose a shift uniform modulo \\(l_0\\) in each oriented axis. For \\(m\\ge0\\), obtain the shift modulo \\(l_{m+1}\\) from that modulo \\(l_m\\) by adding either \\(0\\) or \\(l_m\\), with equal probabilities independently at each step and in each axis. For \\(m&lt;0\\), reduce the initial shift modulo \\(l_m\\). All squares are half-open.\nFor distinct points, let \\(d_j(x,y)\\) be minus the logarithm of the smallest grid side at which their images share a cell. In both cases put\n\\[\nd_j(x,x)=+\\infty,\\qquad\n\\Delta_j(x,y)=d_j(x,y)-i_j(x,y)\\quad(x\\ne y).\n\\]</p><p>For each realized hierarchy, let \\(\\Pi_j(r)\\) be the partition with sharing criterion \\(d_j(x,y)\\ge r\\). A giant is a cell of size greater than \\(n/2\\), denoted \\(G_j(r)\\) when it exists. Replace the giant by its complement, retain all other cells, and keep multiplicities; call the resulting multiset \\(\\mathcal C_j(r)\\). Every occurrence \\(C\\) has \\(\\alpha(C)\\le1/2\\). Define\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_j&amp;=\\sup\\{r:G_j(r)\\text{ exists}\\},\\\\\nS_j(x)&amp;=\\int_{\\{r:G_j(r)\\text{ exists}\\}}\n\\mathbf1_{\\{x\\notin G_j(r)\\}}\\,dr,\\\\\nL_j(x,y)&amp;=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n\\sum_{C\\in\\mathcal C_j(r)}\\mathbf1_C(x)\\mathbf1_C(y)\\,dr\n\\quad(x\\ne y).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor a nonnegative or integrable quantity, write\n\\[\n\\langle H\\rangle=\\sum_v\\sigma_v\\mathbb E_{\\rm grids}H,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal I(g)=\n\\left\\langle\n\\sum_{j=1}^2\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n\\sum_{C\\in\\mathcal C_j(r)}g\\,dr\n\\right\\rangle.\n\\]\nThere is no grid randomness at finite places. All cell sums retain multiplicity.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The quantities\n\\[\nS^*(x)=\\langle S_1(x)+S_2(x)\\rangle,\n\\qquad\nL^*(x,y)=\\langle L_1(x,y)+L_2(x,y)\\rangle\n\\quad(x\\ne y)\n\\]\nare finite, and for an absolute constant \\(\\kappa\\),\n\\[\nL^*(x,y)=b_*+S^*(x)+S^*(y),\n\\qquad\nb_*=2\\kappa-\\langle M_1+M_2\\rangle.\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nConsequently, every signed law on distinct pairs having zero total mass and zero sum of its two marginals has zero integral against \\(L^*\\).</p><p>The following scales are finite and nonnegative:\n\\[\nW_2=\\mathcal I(\\pi(C^2))\n=\\mathbb E_\\pi L^*(x,y),\\qquad\nA=\\mathbb E_\\alpha S^*(x),\\qquad\nW=W_2+A.\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nEvery absolute grid-error bound remains uniform in \\(K\\), since its archimedean weights sum to one.</p>", "proof": "<p>The maps \\(Z_1,Z_2\\) are injective because the original coordinates \\(u,b\\) are real. Their differences remain nonzero under every field embedding. At another complex embedding, the coordinates need not be conjugates; injectivity and (3.1) remain valid.</p><p>The normalized product formula (3.2) uses the stated place weights. For a fixed configuration, only finitely many finite places make any difference \\(Z_j(x)-Z_j(y)\\) a nonunit: each nonzero field element has nonzero valuation at only finitely many prime ideals, and there are finitely many differences.</p><p>At finite places, the ultrametric inequality makes \\(d_j(x,y)\\ge r\\) an equivalence relation. The nested grids give the same property at complex places. Lemmas 2.1 and 2.2 therefore apply. Lemma 2.2 and the finite set of nonunit places show that \\(S^*,L^*\\), and \\(\\langle M_1+M_2\\rangle\\) are finite.</p><p>For each distinct pair, the product formula and Lemma 2.1 give\n\\[\n\\langle d_1(x,y)+d_2(x,y)\\rangle=2\\kappa.\n\\]\nTaking expectations in (3.5) is legitimate and gives\n\\[\nL^*(x,y)\n=\n2\\kappa-\\langle M_1+M_2\\rangle+S^*(x)+S^*(y),\n\\]\nwhich is (3.6). The consequence for signed laws follows immediately from zero total mass and zero sum of marginals. The definitions and nonnegativity give (3.7), including finiteness.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use the coordinate maps and random grids of Proposition 2.", "statement": "<p>Use the coordinate maps and random grids of Proposition 2. At every complex place, the depths are almost surely finite on distinct pairs. The distribution of \\(\\Delta_j(x,y)\\) is independent of the distinct pair, the place, and the configuration. Its finite mean is an absolute constant \\(\\kappa\\), and absolute constants \\(C_0,b_0&gt;0\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\Pr\\{|\\Delta_j(x,y)|&gt;u\\}\\le C_0e^{-b_0u}\n\\quad(u\\ge0).\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nAt finite places, \\(\\Delta_j=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(R&gt;0\\) for the separation of the complex values, and \\((a,b)\\) for their displacement along the oriented axes. At side length \\(l\\), a uniform shift gives sharing probability\n\\[\n(1-|a|/l)_+(1-|b|/l)_+.\n\\]\nSharing is impossible if \\(l&lt;R/\\sqrt2\\), whereas separation has probability at most\n\\[\n\\frac{|a|+|b|}{l}\\le\\frac{\\sqrt2R}{l}.\n\\]\nThe separation events decrease as the grids become coarser, and their probabilities tend to zero. The pair eventually shares a cell almost surely, and there is a smallest sharing scale.</p><p>Write\n\\[\n\\Delta=d_j(x,y)+\\log R.\n\\]\nThe lower bound on a sharing side gives \\(\\Delta\\le\\log\\sqrt2\\). For \\(u\\ge0\\), let \\(l\\) be the largest grid side no larger than \\(Re^u\\), so \\(l&gt;Re^u/2\\). On \\(\\{\\Delta&lt;-u\\}\\), the pair is separated at that side, hence\n\\[\n\\Pr\\{\\Delta&lt;-u\\}\\le2\\sqrt2e^{-u}.\n\\]\nThis proves (3.4) and a uniform first absolute moment.</p><p>For any real \\(u\\), the event \\(\\{\\Delta\\ge u\\}\\) is sharing at the largest side \\(l\\le Re^{-u}\\). By the uniform logarithmic phase, the law of \\(l/R\\) depends only on \\(u\\), and the displacement direction relative to the axes is uniform. The sharing formula makes the entire law of \\(\\Delta\\) independent of the pair.</p><p>The construction uses countably many random variables and cell tests, so the depths and these events are measurable. At finite places the error is zero by definition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each realized hierarchy of Proposition 2 on \\(n\\ge2\\) points, outside the null event of a nonfinite distinct-pair depth, the quantities \\(M_j,S_j(x),L_j(x,y)\\) are finite and, for distinct \\(x,y\\) …", "statement": "<p>For each realized hierarchy of Proposition 2 on \\(n\\ge2\\) points, outside the null event of a nonfinite distinct-pair depth, the quantities \\(M_j,S_j(x),L_j(x,y)\\) are finite and, for distinct \\(x,y\\),\n\\[\nd_j(x,y)=M_j+L_j(x,y)-S_j(x)-S_j(y).\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nThey are integrable over the grids. At every finite place where all distinct coordinate differences are units,\n\\[\nM_j=S_j=L_j=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppress \\(j\\), and let \\(d_-\\) and \\(d_+\\) be the minimum and maximum of the finitely many distinct-pair depths. Below \\(d_-\\), the partition has only the cell \\(P\\); above \\(d_+\\), it has only singletons. A giant at any level has a giant ancestor at every coarser level. Thus a giant exists on an initial ray with endpoint \\(M\\in[d_-,d_+]\\), up to an immaterial endpoint convention.</p><p>Let\n\\[\no(r)=\\mathbf1_{\\{d(x,y)\\ge r\\}},\\qquad\ng(r)=\\mathbf1_{\\{G(r)\\text{ exists}\\}},\\qquad\nt(r)=\\sum_{C\\in\\mathcal C(r)}\\mathbf1_C(x)\\mathbf1_C(y).\n\\]\nExpanding the giant indicator through its complement gives\n\\[\no(r)-g(r)\n=\nt(r)\n-\\mathbf1_{\\{G(r)\\text{ exists},\\,x\\notin G(r)\\}}\n-\\mathbf1_{\\{G(r)\\text{ exists},\\,y\\notin G(r)\\}}.\n\\]\nThe integral of the left side is \\(d(x,y)-M\\). Each term on the right vanishes outside \\([d_-,d_+]\\) on a distinct pair, and \\(t(r)\\le2\\). Integration proves (3.5), with\n\\[\n0\\le S(x)\\le d_+-d_-,\n\\quad\n0\\le L(x,y)\\le2(d_+-d_-),\n\\quad\n|M|\\le\\max\\{|d_-|,|d_+|\\}.\n\\]\nThese bounds are grid integrable by Lemma 2.1, since the configuration has finitely many pairs and finite raw depths.</p><p>At a finite place with all raw depths zero, \\(d_-=d_+=0\\), proving the final assertion. Ties change neither the identity nor the integrals.</p><p>The empty complement of \\(P\\) and singleton occurrences contribute zero to every distinct-pair overlap. No \\(L_j(x,x)\\) is defined: singleton cells persist through an infinite range of levels, so its integral would be infinite.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( W_{2,\\mathrm{sm}}=o(W+1). \\) The estimate holds before passing to a bounded or unbounded subsequence of \\(W\\).", "statement": "<p>Assumptions:\nWork along the sequence of Proposition 1, with fixed \\(c,\\gamma,s,\\theta,B_0\\), its laws \\(\\alpha,\\pi,q,\\mu_e\\), and the hierarchies and finite scales \\(W_2,A,W\\) of Proposition 2. In particular,\n\\[\nq\\le B_0\\pi,\\qquad q_X,q_Y\\le B_0\\alpha,\\qquad\n(q_X+q_Y)/2\\ge(1-o(1))\\alpha,\n\\]\nand the uniform subset estimate (2.4) holds.</p><p>Definitions:\nFor a transformed cell occurrence \\(C\\), put \\(a=\\alpha(C)\\) and \\(p=\\mu_e(C)\\). For \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1/2\\), define\n\\[\nT(\\lambda)=\n\\mathcal I\\!\\left(\n\\mathbb E_e\\mathbf1_C(x_e)p\\mathbf1_{\\{p&lt;\\lambda\\}}\n\\right).\n\\]\nDefine the small-cell overlap\n\\[\nW_{2,\\mathrm{sm}}\n=\n\\mathcal I\\!\\left(\n\\pi(C^2)\\mathbf1_{\\{|C|\\le n^{1-c}\\}}\n\\right).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nW_{2,\\mathrm{sm}}=o(W+1).\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nThe estimate holds before passing to a bounded or unbounded subsequence of \\(W\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Recall \\(\\lambda_n=1/\\log n\\). Apply Lemma 3.2 with\n\\[\n\\tau=\\lambda_n^{1/3},\n\\qquad H=\\lambda_n^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nIt gives\n\\[\nT(\\lambda_n)\n\\ll\n\\lambda_n^{1/3}W+\\lambda_n^{1/2}\n+e^{-b_1\\lambda_n^{-1/2}}\n=o(W+1).\n\\]</p><p>Split \\(\\mathcal I(q(C^2))\\) according to whether \\(\\mu_e(C)&lt;\\lambda_n\\). The first part is bounded by \\(T(\\lambda_n)\\). For the other part, (2.4), uniformly over every subset \\(C\\), gives the upper bound\n\\[\n(1+o(1))(W_2-W_{2,\\mathrm{sm}})\n+o(1)W_{2,\\mathrm{sm}}.\n\\]\nComparing their sum with the lower bound in Lemma 3.1 yields\n\\[\nW_{2,\\mathrm{sm}}=o(W+1).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 3, \\( W_2-o(1)A \\le \\mathcal I(q(C^2)) =\\mathbb E_qL^*(x,y) \\le B_0W_2. \\) Also, \\(T(\\lambda)\\) is finite.", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 3,\n\\[\nW_2-o(1)A\n\\le\n\\mathcal I(q(C^2))\n=\\mathbb E_qL^*(x,y)\n\\le B_0W_2.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nAlso, \\(T(\\lambda)\\) is finite.</p>", "proof": "<p>Equation (3.6) and the uniform marginals of \\(\\pi\\) give\n\\[\nW_2=b_*+2A.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_qL^*(x,y)\n=\nW_2+\n\\sum_{x\\in P}\n\\bigl(q_X(x)+q_Y(x)-2\\alpha(x)\\bigr)S^*(x).\n\\]\nSince \\(S^*\\ge0\\) and \\((q_X+q_Y)/2\\ge(1-o(1))\\alpha\\), the lower bound follows. The upper bound follows from \\(q\\le B_0\\pi\\) and \\(L^*\\ge0\\).</p><p>The integrand defining \\(T(\\lambda)\\) is at most\n\\[\nq(C^2)=\\mathbb E_e\\mathbf1_C(x_e)\\mu_e(C),\n\\]\nso \\(T(\\lambda)&lt;\\infty\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 3, if \\( 0<\\lambda<1/2,\\qquad 2/n\\le\\tau\\le1,\\qquad H\\ge1, \\) then \\( T(\\lambda)\\ll \\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{\\tau^2}+\\lambda\\right)W_2 +\\tau A+\\lambda H+e^{-b_1H}, \\) where \\(b_1>0\\) is …", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 3, if\n\\[\n0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1/2,\\qquad 2/n\\le\\tau\\le1,\\qquad H\\ge1,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nT(\\lambda)\\ll\n\\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{\\tau^2}+\\lambda\\right)W_2\n+\\tau A+\\lambda H+e^{-b_1H},\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(b_1&gt;0\\) is absolute. The implicit constant depends only on \\(B_0\\) and the absolute constants in (3.4).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a set \\(C\\) of size \\(m\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\alpha(C)^2}{2}\n\\le\n\\pi(C^2)=\\frac{m(m-1)}{n(n-1)}\n\\le\\alpha(C)^2.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nIf \\(a\\ge\\tau\\), then \\(m\\ge2\\), and the integrand of \\(T(\\lambda)\\) is at most\n\\[\n\\lambda\\le\\frac{2\\lambda}{\\tau^2}\\pi(C^2).\n\\]\nThese occurrences contribute at most \\(2\\lambda W_2/\\tau^2\\).</p><p>If \\(C\\) is the complement occurrence of a giant and \\(a&lt;\\tau\\), its contribution is at most\n\\[\nq(C^2)\\le B_0\\pi(C^2)\\le B_0\\tau a.\n\\]\nThe integral of \\(a\\) over giant-complement occurrences is \\(A\\), so these contribute at most \\(B_0\\tau A\\). Multiplicities are retained.</p><p>It remains to treat actual nongiant cells of mass less than \\(\\tau\\). Fix a place, a coordinate \\(j\\), and the two entire realized hierarchies. Let \\(j'=3-j\\), and set \\(H_v=H\\) at archimedean places and \\(H_v=0\\) otherwise. Fix a fiber \\(e\\), put \\(x=x_e\\), let \\(U\\) be the actual \\(j\\)-cell containing \\(x\\) at level \\(r\\), and put \\(p=\\mu_e(U)\\). Retain only levels at which \\(U\\) is nongiant, \\(\\alpha(U)&lt;\\tau\\), and \\(p&lt;\\lambda\\).</p><p>Define the source cells in the other coordinate\n\\[\nV_-=\\Pi_{j'}(h_e-r-H_v)(x),\n\\qquad\nV_+=\\Pi_{j'}(h_e-r+H_v)(x).\n\\]\nThus \\(V_+\\subseteq V_-\\). Put\n\\[\nD^-=\\mu_e(P\\setminus(U\\cup V_-)),\n\\qquad\nD^+=\\mu_e(U\\cap V_+).\n\\]</p><p>For a fixed fiber point \\(y\\), write \\(d=d_j(x,y)\\), \\(d'=d_{j'}(x,y)\\). Its contribution to \\(D^-\\) occurs when\n\\[\nd&lt;r&lt;h_e-H_v-d',\n\\]\nand its contribution to \\(D^+\\) occurs when\n\\[\nh_e+H_v-d'\\le r\\le d.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\int D^-\\,dr\n=\\mathbb E_{y\\sim\\mu_e}(h_e-d-d'-H_v)_+,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int D^+\\,dr\n=\\mathbb E_{y\\sim\\mu_e}(d+d'-h_e-H_v)_+.\n\\]\nTheir sum is\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{y\\sim\\mu_e}\n\\bigl(|\\Delta_j(x,y)+\\Delta_{j'}(x,y)|-H_v\\bigr)_+,\n\\]\nby (3.3). At nonarchimedean places it is zero. At archimedean places, (3.4) and the union bound give\n\\[\n\\Pr\\{|\\Delta_j+\\Delta_{j'}|&gt;u\\}\n\\le2C_0e^{-b_0u/2}.\n\\]\nIntegrating the tail, averaging over fibers, and using\n\\(\\sum_{v\\mid\\infty}\\sigma_v=1\\), one obtains\n\\[\n\\left\\langle\n\\mathbb E_e\\int(D^-+D^+)\\,dr\n\\right\\rangle\n\\ll e^{-b_1H}.\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nNo independence of the two errors is required.</p><p>Split the retained levels into three cases. In every change of level, the fiber and both entire realized hierarchies are fixed, so \\(h_e\\) is constant.</p><p>Case 1: \\(V_-\\) is nongiant.\nSince\n\\[\n\\mu_e(V_-)+D^-\\ge1-p&gt;1/2,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\np\\le2\\lambda(\\mu_e(V_-)+D^-).\n\\]\nDiscard the other restrictions and change levels to\n\\[\nr'=h_e-r-H_v.\n\\]\nSharing a nongiant \\(j'\\)-cell contributes to \\(L_{j'}\\), so the main term is at most \\(2\\lambda\\mathbb E_qL_{j'}\\). Summing coordinates and places gives at most \\(2B_0\\lambda W_2\\). Equation (4.4) controls the error, since \\(2\\lambda&lt;1\\).</p><p>Case 2: \\(V_-\\) is giant and \\(V_+\\) is not.\nFor a fixed source, the levels at which its \\(j'\\)-cell is giant form an initial interval. The two defining levels straddle its endpoint, so the allowed \\(r\\) have length at most \\(2H_v\\). Since \\(p&lt;\\lambda\\), this costs at most \\(2\\lambda H_v\\) per fiber; summing and averaging gives at most \\(4\\lambda H\\).</p><p>Case 3: \\(V_+\\) is giant.\nThis is the remaining case, and\n\\[\np=D^++\\mu_e(U\\setminus V_+).\n\\]\nThe first term is controlled by (4.4). For the second, change levels to\n\\[\nr'=h_e-r+H_v.\n\\]\nAt a fixed new level, every counted pair has\n\\[\ny\\notin G_{j'}(r')\n\\]\nand shares an actual \\(j\\)-cell of size less than \\(\\tau n\\) at some original level.</p><p>For a fixed target \\(y\\), let \\(N_j(y)\\) be the union over all levels of the actual \\(j\\)-cells containing \\(y\\) and having size less than \\(\\tau n\\). These cells form a nested chain of subsets of the finite set \\(P\\), so \\(|N_j(y)|&lt;\\tau n\\), with empty union allowed. All possible sources lie in \\(N_j(y)\\setminus\\{y\\}\\), even though their selected levels depend on the fiber. Each edge belongs to exactly one fiber and retains weight \\(1/|E|\\) after changing level. The mass at \\(r'\\) is therefore at most\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\sum_{y\\notin G_{j'}(r')}\n\\sum_{x\\in N_j(y)\\setminus\\{y\\}}q(x,y)\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad\\le\n\\frac{B_0}{n(n-1)}\n\\sum_{\\substack{y\\notin G_{j'}(r')\\\\N_j(y)\\ne\\varnothing}}\n(|N_j(y)|-1)\n\\le\nB_0\\tau\\,\\alpha(P\\setminus G_{j'}(r')).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere \\(|N_j(y)|-1\\le\\tau(n-1)\\), since \\(\\tau\\le1\\). Integrating, summing coordinates, and averaging gives \\(B_0\\tau A\\).</p><p>The three cases, the initial split, and (4.4) prove (4.2). Threshold ties affect only interval endpoints. Every rearranged integrand is nonnegative, so Tonelli's theorem applies.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 5.1, together with its two applications in Section 5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For a constant \\(C_M\\) depending only on \\(M\\) …", "statement": "<p>Assumptions:\nUse the sequence, parameters, laws, hierarchies, and scales of Propositions 1–3. In particular, \\(0&lt;c&lt;\\gamma&lt;s&lt;1\\),\n\\[\nW_{2,\\mathrm{sm}}\n=\\mathcal I\\!\\left(\\pi(C^2)\\mathbf1_{\\{|C|\\le n^{1-c}\\}}\\right)\n=o(W+1).\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a finite family of probability measures \\(\\nu\\), each uniform on a subset \\(B\\subset P\\) of size \\(k\\ge n^\\gamma\\). Give this family nonnegative weights, independent of places and grids, of total mass at most one. Write \\(\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}\\) for the weighted sum, and assume\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}\\nu\\le M\\alpha\n\\]\npointwise, with \\(M\\) fixed. For a transformed cell occurrence \\(C\\), put\n\\[\nm=|C|,\\qquad a=\\alpha(C),\\qquad p=\\nu(C).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For a constant \\(C_M\\) depending only on \\(M\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\mathcal I\\!\\left(\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}\n\\left[\n(p-a)^2\\mathbf1_{\\{m&gt;n^{1-c}\\}}\n+p^2\\mathbf1_{\\{m\\le n^{1-c},\\,p\\ge2/k\\}}\n\\right]\\right)\\\\\n&amp;\\hspace{35mm}\\le C_M\\bigl(n^{c-\\gamma}W_2+W_{2,\\mathrm{sm}}\\bigr)\n=o(W+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(5.1–5.2)\n\\]</p><p>This applies to:\n\\[\n\\nu=\\mu_e,\\quad\\text{with weights }k_e/|E|,\n\\]\nand to\n\\[\n\\nu=\\rho_y,\\quad\\text{with weights }q_Y(y),\n\\quad\\text{retaining only }d^-(y)\\ge n^\\gamma.\n\\]\nThe omitted targets have total \\(q_Y\\)-mass \\(O(n^{\\gamma-1})=o(1)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The integer \\(k\\ge n^\\gamma&gt;1\\) is at least two. Let \\(P_B\\) be uniform on distinct ordered pairs from \\(B\\). Define \\(R_B\\), also on distinct pairs, as follows. First take \\(X\\) uniformly from \\(B\\). If \\(k&lt;n\\), conditionally on \\(X\\), take \\(Y\\) uniformly from \\(B\\setminus\\{X\\}\\) with probability \\(k/n\\), and uniformly from \\(P\\setminus B\\) otherwise. If \\(B=P\\), define \\(R_B=P_B=\\pi\\).</p><p>The first marginal of \\(R_B\\) is \\(\\nu\\), and the second is \\(\\alpha\\). Indeed, \\(y\\in B\\) receives mass\n\\[\n(k-1)\\frac1k\\frac kn\\frac1{k-1}=\\frac1n,\n\\]\nand \\(y\\notin B\\) receives mass\n\\[\nk\\frac1k\\frac{n-k}{n}\\frac1{n-k}=\\frac1n.\n\\]\nThe signed measure \\(P_B-2R_B+\\pi\\) has total mass zero, with first and second marginals \\(\\alpha-\\nu\\) and \\(\\nu-\\alpha\\). Their sum is zero. The additive identity (3.6) gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal I\\bigl(P_B(C^2)-2R_B(C^2)+\\pi(C^2)\\bigr)=0.\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nAll three nonnegative terms have finite integrals: for fixed \\(n\\), any probability law on distinct pairs is bounded by \\(n(n-1)\\pi\\). The family weights are independent of places and grids, so (5.3) may be averaged over \\(\\mathcal F\\).</p><p>Write \\(b=|B\\cap C|\\), so \\(p=b/k\\). Then\n\\[\nP_B(C^2)\n=\\frac{b(b-1)}{k(k-1)}\n=p^2-\\frac{p(1-p)}{k-1},\n\\]\n\\[\nR_B(C^2)\n=p\\left(\\frac kn\\frac{b-1}{k-1}+\\frac{m-b}{n}\\right)\n=ap-\\frac kn\\frac{p(1-p)}{k-1},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\pi(C^2)\n=\\frac{m(m-1)}{n(n-1)}\n=a^2-\\frac{a(1-a)}{n-1}.\n\\]\nThe middle identity also holds when \\(k=n\\). Thus, with\n\\[\nH_B(C)=P_B(C^2)-2R_B(C^2)+\\pi(C^2),\n\\]\none has\n\\[\nH_B(C)\n=(p-a)^2+\n\\left(\\frac{2k}{n}-1\\right)\\frac{p(1-p)}{k-1}\n-\\frac{a(1-a)}{n-1}.\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\n\\overline H=\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}H_B,\\qquad\n\\omega=\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}1\\le1.\n\\]\nIn the averaged formula, the last term of (5.4) is multiplied by \\(\\omega\\).</p><p>Large cells:\nSuppose \\(m&gt;n^{1-c}\\). Since\n\\[\n\\frac1{k-1}\\le2n^{-\\gamma},\n\\qquad\n\\frac1{n-1}\\le2n^{-\\gamma},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}p\\le Ma,\n\\]\nequation (5.4) implies\n\\[\n\\overline H(C)\n\\ge\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}(p-a)^2\n-2(M+1)n^{-\\gamma}a.\n\\]\nFor every \\(m\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n\\frac12a^2\\le\\pi(C^2)\\le a^2.\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nHere \\(a&gt;n^{-c}\\), so\n\\[\nn^{-\\gamma}a\\le2n^{c-\\gamma}\\pi(C^2).\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\overline H(C)\n\\ge\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}(p-a)^2\n-4(M+1)n^{c-\\gamma}\\pi(C^2).\n\\tag{5.6}\n\\]</p><p>Small nonsingleton cells:\nSuppose \\(2\\le m\\le n^{1-c}\\). If \\(p\\ge2/k\\), then \\(b\\ge2\\), and\n\\[\nP_B(C^2)\n=p^2\\frac{k}{k-1}\\frac{b-1}{b}\n\\ge\\frac12p^2.\n\\]\nSince \\(P_B(C^2)\\ge0\\) always,\n\\[\nP_B(C^2)\\ge\\frac12p^2\\mathbf1_{\\{p\\ge2/k\\}}.\n\\]\nAlso \\(R_B(C^2)\\le ap\\), and therefore\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}R_B(C^2)\n\\le Ma^2\\le2M\\pi(C^2).\n\\]\nDiscarding \\(\\omega\\pi(C^2)\\ge0\\) gives\n\\[\n\\overline H(C)\n\\ge\n\\frac12\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}\n\\left[p^2\\mathbf1_{\\{p\\ge2/k\\}}\\right]\n-4M\\pi(C^2).\n\\tag{5.7}\n\\]</p><p>Empty and singleton cells:\nIf \\(m=0\\) or \\(m=1\\), all three pair probabilities vanish exactly. For a singleton, \\(p\\) is zero or \\(1/k\\), so the small-cell term in the conclusion also vanishes. This exact cancellation treats the infinite singleton tails. The separate correction terms in (5.4) are never integrated over those tails.</p><p>Integrate (5.6) and (5.7) on their respective regions, use exact zero on empty and singleton cells, and apply (5.3). The result is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\mathcal I\\!\\left(\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}\n\\left[\n(p-a)^2\\mathbf1_{\\{m&gt;n^{1-c}\\}}\n+\\tfrac12p^2\\mathbf1_{\\{m\\le n^{1-c},\\,p\\ge2/k\\}}\n\\right]\\right)\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad\\le\n4(M+1)n^{c-\\gamma}W_2+4MW_{2,\\mathrm{sm}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe nonnegative left-hand terms are integrable by the pointwise bounds and integrability of \\(\\overline H\\) and the errors. Doubling proves (5.1) with \\(C_M=8(M+1)\\). All cell estimates hold for arbitrary subsets, including giant complements and repeated occurrences. If \\(B=P\\), the signed expression and large-cell variance are zero; the small-cell term may be \\(a^2\\), absorbed by \\(2\\pi(C^2)\\). Since \\(c&lt;\\gamma\\), \\(W_2\\le W\\), and Proposition 3 applies, the bound is \\(o(W+1)\\).</p><p>Applications:\nFor fiber laws, the weights sum to one and\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_e\\mu_e=q_Y\\le B_0\\alpha.\n\\]\nTheir support sizes satisfy \\(k_e\\ge n^{s-o(1)}\\ge n^\\gamma\\) eventually.</p><p>For incoming laws, write \\(d_y=d^-(y)\\), retain \\(d_y\\ge n^\\gamma\\), and use weights \\(q_Y(y)=d_y/|E|\\). Then\n\\[\n\\sum_{y:d_y\\ge n^\\gamma}q_Y(y)\\rho_y\n\\le q_X\\le B_0\\alpha.\n\\]\nEach retained law is uniform on \\(d_y\\) sources, and the family weights sum to at most one. The omitted mass satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum_{y:d_y&lt;n^\\gamma}q_Y(y)\n\\le\\frac{n^{1+\\gamma}}{|E|}\n=O(n^{\\gamma-1})=o(1).\n\\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nBoth families are defined by the same graph at every place, as required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is no subsequence of this sequence on which \\(W\\to\\infty\\).", "statement": "<p>Assumptions:\nLet \\(P,E\\) be the sequence prepared in Proposition 1, with its graph laws and fixed parameters \\(0&lt;c&lt;\\gamma&lt;s&lt;1\\). Use the arithmetic hierarchies, additive overlap identity, and scales \\(W_2,A,W\\) of Proposition 2. The small-cell and variance estimates of Propositions 3 and 4 apply to the fiber laws and to incoming laws with incoming degree at least \\(n^\\gamma\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is no subsequence of this sequence on which \\(W\\to\\infty\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(W\\to\\infty\\). Choose \\(\\delta,w\\) using Lemma 5.1. In the tree for \\(D_j\\), every positive edge has both side masses greater than \\(\\delta/2\\), by the support condition on \\(w\\). Apply Lemma 5.4 with \\(L=\\ell_j\\), sum over \\(j=1,2\\), and average over places and grids. Lemmas 5.1 and 5.3 give\n\\[\nW\\ll\\langle\\ell_1+\\ell_2\\rangle\n\\ll_\\delta\n\\sum_{j=1}^2\n\\left\\langle\n\\mathbb E_{\\alpha^{\\otimes3}}\n|D_j(x,y)-D_j(x',y)|\n\\right\\rangle\n=o(W),\n\\]\na contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a fixed \\(c_1>0\\) such that \\( W_2\\ge c_1W \\) eventually.", "statement": "<p>Assume the setting of Proposition 5 and, additionally, \\(W\\to\\infty\\). Thus every \\(o(W+1)\\) estimate below is \\(o(W)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a fixed \\(c_1&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\nW_2\\ge c_1W\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\neventually. A fixed sufficiently small \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1/4\\) can be chosen so that\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\mathbf1_{\\{a&gt;\\delta\\}})\\gg W,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal I(\\mathbf1_{\\{a\\ge\\delta/4\\}})\\ll_\\delta W,\n\\quad a=\\alpha(C).\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]</p><p>Choose a Lipschitz function \\(w:[0,1]\\to[0,1]\\) satisfying\n\\[\nw(u)=w(1-u),\\qquad\nw=0\\text{ on }[0,\\delta/2]\\cup[1-\\delta/2,1],\n\\qquad\nw=1\\text{ on }[\\delta,1-\\delta].\n\\]\nFor the original partitions, define\n\\[\n\\ell_j=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n\\sum_{B\\in\\Pi_j(r)}w(\\alpha(B))\\,dr.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nW\\ll\\langle\\ell_1+\\ell_2\\rangle\\ll_\\delta W.\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]\nFor either family from Proposition 4,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Bigg\\langle\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}\\sum_{j=1}^2\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n\\sum_{B\\in\\Pi_j(r)}\n\\big[\n&amp;|w(\\nu(B))-w(\\alpha(B))|\\\\\n&amp;+w(\\alpha(B))|\\nu(B)-\\alpha(B)|\n\\big]\\,dr\n\\Bigg\\rangle=o(W).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(6.7)\n\\]\nFor incoming laws, the family in (6.7) retains only targets of incoming degree at least \\(n^\\gamma\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At a fixed place and choice of grids, abbreviate\n\\[\nS=S_1+S_2,\\qquad L=L_1+L_2.\n\\]\nChoose \\(e\\) with its edge-induced weight, then choose \\(Y,Y'\\) independently with law \\(\\mu_e\\). Equations (3.3) and (3.5) give\n\\[\nS(Y)=M_1+M_2-S(x_e)-h_e+L(x_e,Y)\n-\\Delta_1(x_e,Y)-\\Delta_2(x_e,Y).\n\\]\nAll source-neighbor pairs are distinct. The first-moment bound from (3.4), the archimedean normalization, and (4.1) imply\n\\[\n\\left\\langle\\mathbb E|S(Y)-S(Y')|\\right\\rangle\n\\le2\\langle\\mathbb E_qL(x,y)\\rangle+O(1)\n=O(W_2)+O(1).\n\\]</p><p>For every vertex,\n\\[\nS^*(x)\\ge A-2W_2\\qquad(n&gt;2).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nIndeed, the average of \\(L^*(x,y)\\) over \\(y\\ne x\\) is\n\\[\nW_2+\\frac{n-2}{n-1}(S^*(x)-A),\n\\]\nand is nonnegative. Hence\n\\[\n\\langle\\mathbb ES(Y)\\rangle\\ge A-2W_2,\n\\]\nand therefore\n\\[\n\\left\\langle\\mathbb E\\min\\{S(Y),S(Y')\\}\\right\\rangle\n\\ge A-O(W_2)-O(1).\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]</p><p>For two nonnegative lists \\((a_1,a_2)\\), \\((b_1,b_2)\\),\n\\[\n\\min\\{a_1+a_2,b_1+b_2\\}\n\\le\\sum_{i,j=1}^2\\min\\{a_i,b_j\\}.\n\\]\nIf the first sum is smaller, then\n\\(\\sum_j\\min\\{a_i,b_j\\}\\ge a_i\\) for each \\(i\\).</p><p>Nesting of giants gives, for almost every \\(u&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\{z:S_i(z)&gt;u\\}=P\\setminus G_i(M_i-u).\n\\]\nA vertex leaving the giant at level \\(r_z&lt;M_i\\) has \\(S_i(z)=M_i-r_z\\); one never leaving has \\(S_i(z)=0\\). Only finitely many threshold ties occur. Conditional independence of \\(Y,Y'\\) and\n\\(ab\\le(a^2+b^2)/2\\) now give\n\\[\n\\left\\langle\\mathbb E\\min\\{S(Y),S(Y')\\}\\right\\rangle\n\\le\n2\\left\\langle\n\\sum_{i=1}^2\\mathbb E_e\n\\int_{r&lt;M_i}\\mu_e(P\\setminus G_i(r))^2\\,dr\n\\right\\rangle.\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]</p><p>For a giant-complement occurrence, write \\(p=\\mu_e(C)\\), \\(a=\\alpha(C)\\). If \\(|C|&gt;n^{1-c}\\), use\n\\[\np^2\\le2(p-a)^2+2a^2,\n\\]\nProposition 4, and \\(a^2\\le2\\pi(C^2)\\). For smaller complements with \\(p\\ge2/k_e\\), use the small-cell term in Proposition 4. The remaining terms obey\n\\[\np^2\\le2n^{-\\gamma}p.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mathbb E_ep=q_Y(C)\\le B_0a\\), their integral is \\(O(n^{-\\gamma}A)\\), charged to the finite integral of complement masses. Thus\n\\[\n\\left\\langle\\mathbb E\\min\\{S(Y),S(Y')\\}\\right\\rangle\n\\le O(W_2)+o(W)+O(n^{-\\gamma}A).\n\\]\nTogether with (6.3), this gives\n\\[\nA\\le O(W_2)+o(W)+O(1),\n\\]\nand hence (6.1). Equation (4.1) also gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(q(C^2))\\gg W.\n\\]</p><p>For a fixed \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1/4\\), split the contribution from \\(a\\le\\delta\\) according to \\(p&lt;2\\delta\\) or \\(p\\ge2\\delta\\). In the latter case,\n\\[\np\\le\\frac{p^2}{2\\delta},\n\\qquad p-a\\ge p/2.\n\\]\nThe first variance term controls large cells. On small cells, eventually \\(p\\ge2\\delta\\ge2/k_e\\), so the second variance term applies. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\mathbf1_{\\{a\\le\\delta\\}}q(C^2))\n\\le T(2\\delta)+o_\\delta(W)\n\\le O(\\delta^{1/3}W)+o_\\delta(W).\n\\]\nFor the final inequality, use Lemma 3.2 with\n\\[\n\\lambda=2\\delta,\\qquad\\tau=\\lambda^{1/3},\n\\]\nand any fixed \\(H\\ge1\\). Terms depending only on \\(\\delta,H\\) are \\(o_\\delta(W)\\), and the constant in \\(O(\\delta^{1/3}W)\\) is independent of \\(\\delta\\).</p><p>Choose \\(\\delta\\) sufficiently small once and for all, and then let \\(n\\to\\infty\\). Since \\(q(C^2)\\le1\\), the first part of (6.5) follows. For the second, \\(a\\ge\\delta/4\\) implies, for large \\(n\\),\n\\[\n\\pi(C^2)\\ge a^2/2\\ge\\delta^2/32.\n\\]\nThis proves (6.5).</p><p>Replacing a giant by its complement preserves every summand \\(w(\\alpha(B))\\), by symmetry, with occurrences counted separately. Equations (6.5) and the support and value conditions on \\(w\\) yield (6.6).</p><p>To prove (6.7), its integrand is invariant under\n\\[\n(p,a)\\longmapsto(1-p,1-a).\n\\]\nWork with transformed cells. On \\(a\\ge\\delta/4\\), all cells are large for large \\(n\\); the Lipschitz bound, Cauchy–Schwarz, (6.5), and Proposition 4 give \\(o(W)\\). On \\(a&lt;\\delta/4\\), the second summand vanishes, and the first can be nonzero only if \\(p&gt;\\delta/2\\). For large cells,\n\\[\n1\\le16\\delta^{-2}(p-a)^2;\n\\]\nfor small cells,\n\\[\n1\\le4\\delta^{-2}p^2,\n\\qquad p\\ge2/k\n\\]\neventually. Proposition 4 again gives \\(o(W)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "These quantities are finite for sufficiently large \\(n\\).", "statement": "<p>Assume the setting of Proposition 5, \\(W\\to\\infty\\), and choose \\(\\delta,w\\) as in Lemma 5.1.</p><p>For \\(\\nu=\\alpha\\) or a fiber law \\(\\mu_e\\), define\n\\[\nD_j^\\nu(a_0,a_1)\n=\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\sum_{B\\in\\Pi_j(r)}\n|\\mathbf1_B(a_0)-\\mathbf1_B(a_1)|w(\\nu(B))\\,dr.\n\\]\nAllow a formal endpoint \\(\\infty\\), with \\(\\mathbf1_B(\\infty)=0\\) for every cell. Put \\(D_j=D_j^\\alpha\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These quantities are finite for sufficiently large \\(n\\). For distinct \\(a_0,a_1\\in P\\),\n\\[\nD_j^\\nu(a_0,a_1)\n=\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}\nw\\!\\left(\n\\nu\\{z:d_j(a_1,z)-d_j(a_0,z)\\ge u\\}\n\\right)\\,du,\n\\tag{6.8}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nD_j^\\nu(\\infty,y)\n=\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}\nw\\!\\left(\\nu\\{z:d_j(y,z)\\ge u\\}\\right)\\,du.\n\\tag{6.9}\n\\]\nFor \\(j'=3-j\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\langle\n\\mathbb E_q|D_j(x,y)-D_{j'}(\\infty,y)|\n\\right\\rangle=o(W).\n\\tag{6.11}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On the initial ray, the only cell is \\(P\\), with weight \\(w(1)=0\\). On the final ray, cells are singletons of \\(\\nu\\)-mass \\(0,1/n\\), or \\(1/k_e\\), so their weights vanish for sufficiently large \\(n\\). Thus the distances are finite.</p><p>For (6.8), put \\(d_0=d_j(a_0,a_1)\\). Nestedness implies that\n\\[\nd_j(a_1,z)&gt;d_0\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ d_j(a_0,z)=d_0,\n\\]\nand conversely with indices exchanged. Otherwise the depths to \\(z\\) agree. For \\(u&gt;0\\), the event in (6.8) is the \\(a_1\\)-cell at level \\(d_0+u\\). For \\(u&lt;0\\), its complement is the \\(a_0\\)-cell at level \\(d_0-u\\), up to strict versus weak threshold ties. There are finitely many finite depths, so these ties have zero Lebesgue measure. Split the integral at zero and use \\(w(t)=w(1-t)\\); this gives exactly the two cell contributions above common depth \\(d_0\\). The depth difference is \\(+\\infty\\) at \\(z=a_1\\) and \\(-\\infty\\) at \\(z=a_0\\); no \\(\\infty-\\infty\\) occurs. Equation (6.9) follows directly from the cell definition.</p><p>For an edge \\((x,y)\\) in fiber \\(e\\), the fiber equation gives, for \\(z\\in F_e\\),\n\\[\nZ_{j'}(z)=Z_{j'}(x)+\\frac{t_e}{Z_j(z)-Z_j(x)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(z\\ne y\\) in this fiber,\n\\[\nZ_{j'}(z)-Z_{j'}(y)\n=\n\\frac{t_e(Z_j(y)-Z_j(z))}\n{(Z_j(z)-Z_j(x))(Z_j(y)-Z_j(x))}.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\ni_{j'}(y,z)\n=\ni_j(y,z)-i_j(x,z)+h_e-i_j(x,y).\n\\tag{6.10}\n\\]</p><p>Under \\(z\\sim\\mu_e\\), compare\n\\[\nU(z)=d_{j'}(y,z),\n\\qquad\nV(z)=d_j(y,z)-d_j(x,z)+h_e-i_j(x,y).\n\\]\nFor \\(z\\ne y\\), (6.10) implies\n\\[\n|U(z)-V(z)|\n\\le\n|\\Delta_{j'}(y,z)|+|\\Delta_j(y,z)|+|\\Delta_j(x,z)|.\n\\]\nAt \\(z=y\\), both variables are \\(+\\infty\\), so their indicators agree at every finite threshold; \\(z=x\\) has zero \\(\\mu_e\\)-mass. Tonelli's theorem gives\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n|\\Pr(U\\ge u)-\\Pr(V\\ge u)|\\,du\n\\le\n\\sum_{z\\in F_e\\setminus\\{y\\}}\n\\mu_e(z)|U(z)-V(z)|.\n\\]\nTranslation by \\(h_e-i_j(x,y)\\) does not change the integral of the weighted tail. The Lipschitz bound for \\(w\\), (6.8), and (6.9), followed by averaging, give\n\\[\n\\left\\langle\n\\mathbb E_q\n|D_j^{\\mu_e}(x,y)-D_{j'}^{\\mu_e}(\\infty,y)|\n\\right\\rangle=O_\\delta(1).\n\\]\nThe grid first moment is uniformly bounded for every distinct pair by (3.4). All vertex laws are independent of grids, nonarchimedean errors vanish, and archimedean weights sum to one.</p><p>Changing \\(\\mu_e\\) to \\(\\alpha\\) changes any one cut distance by at most\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\sum_{B\\in\\Pi_j(r)}\n|w(\\mu_e(B))-w(\\alpha(B))|\\,dr,\n\\]\nindependently of endpoints. Equation (6.7) therefore gives (6.11).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Each \\(D_j\\) is the path pseudometric of a finite weighted tree of total edge length \\(\\ell_j\\), with the formal endpoint represented by its root.", "statement": "<p>Assume the setting and definitions of Lemma 5.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each \\(D_j\\) is the path pseudometric of a finite weighted tree of total edge length \\(\\ell_j\\), with the formal endpoint represented by its root. For probabilities \\(\\rho,\\alpha\\) on its leaves, let \\(T_j(\\rho,\\alpha)\\) be the least expected \\(D_j\\)-distance over couplings. Then\n\\[\nT_j(\\rho,\\alpha)\n=\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\sum_{B\\in\\Pi_j(r)}\nw(\\alpha(B))|\\rho(B)-\\alpha(B)|\\,dr.\n\\tag{6.12}\n\\]\nFurthermore,\n\\[\n\\langle T_j(q_Y,\\alpha)\\rangle=o(W),\n\\qquad\n\\left\\langle\\mathbb E_{q_Y}T_j(\\rho_y,\\alpha)\\right\\rangle=o(W),\n\\tag{6.13}\n\\]\nand, for independent \\(x,x',y\\) with law \\(\\alpha\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\langle\n\\mathbb E_{\\alpha^{\\otimes3}}\n|D_j(x,y)-D_j(x',y)|\n\\right\\rangle=o(W).\n\\tag{6.14}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the distinct cells of the nested partitions as vertices, root the inclusion tree at \\(P\\), and connect each proper cell \\(B\\) to its least strict supercell. Give this edge length\n\\[\nl_j(B)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n\\mathbf1_{\\{B\\in\\Pi_j(r)\\}}w(\\alpha(B))\\,dr.\n\\]\nPoints of \\(P\\) are singleton leaves. An edge is crossed by their connecting path exactly when its cell separates them, proving the cut formula for \\(D_j\\). The total edge length is \\(\\ell_j\\). The formal endpoint is represented by the root, since the root-cell weight is zero.</p><p>Across the edge above \\(B\\), any coupling must move at least\n\\[\n|\\rho(B)-\\alpha(B)|\n\\]\nunits of mass. Conversely, match common mass at each leaf, then work upward, matching surplus mass from child subtrees to deficits in other child subtrees, and send only the remaining imbalance to the parent. Across each edge, the moved mass is precisely its absolute imbalance; the root imbalance is zero. Decomposing these finitely many transfers into leaf-to-leaf paths gives a coupling attaining\n\\[\nT_j(\\rho,\\alpha)\n=\\sum_{B\\ne P}l_j(B)|\\rho(B)-\\alpha(B)|,\n\\]\nwhich is (6.12). Zero-length edges do not affect the argument.</p><p>Since \\(q_Y=\\mathbb E_e\\mu_e\\), convexity and (6.7) give the first part of (6.13). Apply (6.7) to incoming laws at targets of degree at least \\(n^\\gamma\\) for the second. The omitted \\(q_Y\\)-mass is \\(O(n^{\\gamma-1})=o(1)\\), independent of the hierarchy. Every transport cost is at most \\(\\ell_j\\), so the omitted contribution is\n\\[\no(1)\\langle\\ell_j\\rangle=o(W)\n\\]\nby (6.6). This proves (6.13).</p><p>Fix a hierarchy and put\n\\[\nF(x,x',y)=|D_j(x,y)-D_j(x',y)|.\n\\]\nIf \\(Y\\sim q_Y\\) and \\(X,X'\\) are conditionally independent with law \\(\\rho_Y\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb EF(X,X',Y)\n\\le\n2\\mathbb E_q|D_j(x,y)-D_{j'}(\\infty,y)|.\n\\]\nFor each \\(y\\), use two independent copies of an optimal coupling of \\(\\rho_y\\) to \\(\\alpha\\), replacing \\(X,X'\\) by \\(U,U'\\). Conditionally on \\(y\\), the new points are independent with law \\(\\alpha\\); since this law does not depend on \\(y\\), they are also independent of \\(Y\\). The triangle inequality bounds the change in \\(F\\) by the two transport distances.</p><p>Next optimally couple \\(Y\\sim q_Y\\) to \\(V\\sim\\alpha\\), independently of \\(U,U'\\). Moving the last argument costs at most twice its transport distance. Thus\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_{\\alpha^{\\otimes3}}F\n\\le{}&amp;\n2\\mathbb E_q|D_j(x,y)-D_{j'}(\\infty,y)|\\\\\n&amp;+2\\mathbb E_{q_Y}T_j(\\rho_y,\\alpha)\n+2T_j(q_Y,\\alpha).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe right side consists of explicit finite cut sums, so no selection of couplings across places or grids is required. Integrate, and use (6.11) and (6.13), to obtain (6.14).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "Lemma 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\mathbb E|d(X,Y)-d(X',Y)| \\ge \\frac{\\delta^2}{32(\\lceil16/\\delta\\rceil+1)}\\,L. \\)", "statement": "<p>Assumptions:\nLet a finite tree have nonnegative edge lengths of total sum \\(L\\), path pseudometric \\(d\\), and a probability \\(\\alpha\\) on its vertices. Suppose every positive-length edge separates two sets each having \\(\\alpha\\)-mass at least \\(\\delta/2\\), where \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1\\). Let \\(X,X',Y\\) be independent with law \\(\\alpha\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathbb E|d(X,Y)-d(X',Y)|\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\delta^2}{32(\\lceil16/\\delta\\rceil+1)}\\,L.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>There is nothing to prove if \\(L=0\\). For each fixed \\(y\\), the expected distance from \\(X\\) to \\(y\\) is at least \\(\\delta L/2\\): every positive edge separates \\(y\\) from mass at least \\(\\delta/2\\). Since distances are at most \\(L\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr\\{d(X,y)\\ge\\delta L/4\\}\\ge\\delta/4.\n\\]</p><p>Traverse the tree in a closed walk crossing every edge twice, of length \\(2L\\). Partition the walk into at most\n\\[\nM=\\lceil16/\\delta\\rceil+1\n\\]\nintervals of length at most \\(\\delta L/8\\), and assign each vertex to an interval containing one chosen visit. The resulting classes \\(C_r\\) have diameter at most \\(\\delta L/8\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\Pr\\{d(X',Y)\\le\\delta L/8\\}\n\\ge\\sum_{r=1}^M\\alpha(C_r)^2\n\\ge1/M.\n\\]</p><p>Conditionally on \\(Y\\), the far event for \\(X\\) and near event for \\(X'\\) are independent. The far probability is uniformly at least \\(\\delta/4\\), so both occur with probability at least \\(\\delta/(4M)\\). On their intersection the absolute distance difference is at least \\(\\delta L/8\\). The asserted bound follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is no subsequence on which \\(W\\) remains bounded.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions:\nLet \\(P,E\\) be the sequence prepared in Proposition 1, with fixed parameters and graph laws. Use the arithmetic hierarchies, overlap identity, and scales \\(W_2,A,W\\) from Proposition 2. Propositions 3 and 4 apply to the fiber laws and the retained incoming laws.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is no subsequence on which \\(W\\) remains bounded.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(W\\) stays bounded along a subsequence. Lemma 6.1 selects complex embeddings and normalized coordinate laws with nonatomic limits \\(\\beta_1,\\beta_2\\). Lemma 6.2 identifies these as the limits of typical fiber laws and incoming conditional laws. Lemma 6.3 then produces a law \\(Q\\) on finite quadruples with all the properties excluded by Lemma 6.4. This contradiction rules out a bounded-\\(W\\) subsequence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each \\(n\\), one can choose an archimedean place \\(v\\) such that the local scale \\( T_v= \\mathbb E_{\\rm grids}\\sum_{j=1}^2 \\bigl(\\mathbb E_\\pi L_j+\\mathbb E_\\alpha S_j\\bigr) \\) is uniformly bounded, while the local …", "statement": "<p>Assume the setting of Proposition 6 and, additionally, that \\(W\\) is bounded.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each \\(n\\), one can choose an archimedean place \\(v\\) such that the local scale\n\\[\nT_v=\n\\mathbb E_{\\rm grids}\\sum_{j=1}^2\n\\bigl(\\mathbb E_\\pi L_j+\\mathbb E_\\alpha S_j\\bigr)\n\\]\nis uniformly bounded, while the local sum \\(V_v\\) of the left-hand variance integrals in Proposition 4, for the fiber and retained incoming families, tends to zero. The local integrals include both coordinates and grid expectations but omit \\(\\sigma_v\\).</p><p>At these selected embeddings, there are constants \\(m_j\\in\\mathbb R\\), translations \\(b_j\\in\\mathbb C\\), and normalized coordinates\n\\[\nU_j(x)=e^{m_j}Z_j(x)+b_j,\\qquad j=1,2,\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\pi\n\\left|\\log|U_j(x)-U_j(y)|\\right|\n\\le C_1\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nuniformly. If\n\\[\n\\alpha_n^j=(U_j)_*\\alpha,\n\\]\nthen, after passage to a common subsequence,\n\\[\n\\alpha_n^j\\Rightarrow\\beta_j\n\\]\non the Riemann sphere \\(S=\\widehat{\\mathbb C}\\), where each \\(\\beta_j\\) is a nonatomic probability measure on \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(W\\) is bounded, Proposition 4 gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{v\\mid\\infty}\\sigma_vT_v\\le C,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{v\\mid\\infty}\\sigma_vV_v=\\eta_n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThe archimedean weights sum to one. The places with \\(T_v\\le2C+1\\) have total weight at least \\(1/2\\), so one has \\(V_v\\le2\\eta_n\\). Choose such a place for each \\(n\\). No lower bound on an individual place weight is required. All coordinates and grids in the rest of this lemma refer to the selected embedding.</p><p>Equation (3.5) and (3.4) imply\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_{\\rm grids}\\mathbb E_\\pi|i_j(x,y)-M_j|\n\\le\n\\mathbb E_{\\rm grids}\\mathbb E_\\pi\n\\bigl(\nL_j(x,y)+S_j(x)+S_j(y)+|\\Delta_j(x,y)|\n\\bigr)\n=O(1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nm_j=\\mathbb E_\\pi i_j(x,y).\n\\]\nFor each realized grid,\n\\[\n|m_j-M_j|\\le\\mathbb E_\\pi|i_j-M_j|,\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\pi|i_j-m_j|=O(1).\n\\]\nThe normalization \\(U_j=e^{m_j}Z_j+b_j\\) gives (7.1), independently of the translation.</p><p>Equation (7.1) implies\n\\[\n(\\alpha_n^j\\otimes\\alpha_n^j)\n\\{(z,z'):|z-z'|&gt;R\\}\n\\le\\frac{C_1}{\\log R}\n\\quad(R&gt;1).\n\\]\nChoose a fixed \\(R_0&gt;1\\) making this less than \\(1/2\\). Averaging over a center at an empirical point gives a radius-\\(R_0\\) disk containing at least half the mass. Choose \\(b_j\\) to move its center to zero.</p><p>For \\(R&gt;2R_0\\), pairs between this disk and the complement of the radius-\\(R\\) disk have distance at least \\(R/2\\), so\n\\[\n\\alpha_n^j\\{|z|&gt;R\\}\n\\le\\frac{2C_1}{\\log(R/2)}.\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]\nFor every \\(z_0\\in\\mathbb C\\) and \\(0&lt;r&lt;1/2\\), similarly,\n\\[\n\\alpha_n^j(B(z_0,r))^2\n\\le\\frac1n+\\frac{C_1}{\\log(1/(2r))}.\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]\nIndeed, the diagonal mass of independent pairs in the ball is at most \\(1/n\\), and every remaining pair has distance at most \\(2r\\).</p><p>Give \\(S=\\widehat{\\mathbb C}\\) its chordal metric. Probability measures on a compact metric space form a compact metrizable space for weak convergence. Pass to a common subsequence with \\(\\alpha_n^j\\Rightarrow\\beta_j\\) on \\(S\\).</p><p>Apply the open-set inequality in Portmanteau to the spherical open sets\n\\[\n\\{|z|&gt;R\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\n\\]\nin (7.2), then let \\(R\\to\\infty\\). This gives \\(\\beta_j\\{\\infty\\}=0\\). Apply it to open disks in (7.3), then let \\(r\\downarrow0\\). Every \\(\\beta_j\\) is nonatomic.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(\\varphi\\in C(S)\\), \\( \\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F} \\left| \\int\\varphi\\,d\\nu_n^j-\\int\\varphi\\,d\\beta_j \\right|\\longrightarrow0. \\) For incoming laws, the conclusion also holds in full \\(q_Y\\)-expectation after …", "statement": "<p>Assume the bounded-\\(W\\) setting and the selected embeddings, normalizations, and limits of Lemma 6.1.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal F\\) be either:\nthe fiber family with weights \\(k_e/|E|\\), or the incoming family restricted to \\(d^-(y)\\ge n^\\gamma\\), with weights \\(q_Y(y)\\). For a member \\(\\nu\\), put\n\\[\n\\nu_n^j=(U_j)_*\\nu.\n\\]\nThe family weights have total mass at most one, and\n\\(\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}\\nu\\le B\\alpha\\) for a fixed \\(B\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\varphi\\in C(S)\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}\n\\left|\n\\int\\varphi\\,d\\nu_n^j-\\int\\varphi\\,d\\beta_j\n\\right|\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]\nFor incoming laws, the conclusion also holds in full \\(q_Y\\)-expectation after restoring the omitted targets.</p>", "proof": "<p>Push each realized grid through the same normalization\n\\[\nz\\longmapsto e^{m_j}z+b_j.\n\\]\nThis shifts levels by \\(-m_j\\) and leaves integration over all levels unchanged.</p><p>Fix \\(R&gt;0\\) and \\(0&lt;\\epsilon&lt;1\\). At normalized levels\n\\[\nI_\\epsilon=[-\\log\\epsilon,-\\log\\epsilon+1],\n\\]\nactual square cells have sides between \\(\\epsilon/(2e)\\) and \\(\\epsilon\\). At most \\(N=N(R,\\epsilon)\\) squares meet the radius-\\(R\\) disk, uniformly in grid and level.</p><p>For each such actual cell \\(B'\\), use its transformed occurrence \\(C=B'\\), unless \\(B'\\) is giant, in which case \\(C=P\\setminus B'\\). Both measures have total mass one, so\n\\[\n|\\nu(B')-\\alpha(B')|=|\\nu(C)-\\alpha(C)|.\n\\]\nOccurrences retain multiplicity. For \\(|C|&gt;n^{1-c}\\), Cauchy–Schwarz over the family, grids, unit level interval, and at most \\(N\\) occurrences bounds the integrated sum of mean absolute discrepancies by \\(\\sqrt{NV_v}\\). For the other occurrences, including an empty giant complement,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}|\\nu(C)-\\alpha(C)|\n\\le(B+1)\\alpha(C)\n\\le(B+1)n^{-c}.\n\\]</p><p>Approximate \\(\\varphi\\) on these squares by its value at any point of the square. Its oscillation is at most\n\\(\\omega_\\varphi(\\sqrt2\\epsilon)\\), where \\(\\omega_\\varphi\\) is a uniform modulus of continuity in the spherical metric. The remaining squares lie outside the radius-\\(R\\) disk, and their mean family mass is at most \\(B\\alpha_n^j\\{|z|&gt;R\\}\\).</p><p>Writing \\(\\mu\\varphi=\\int\\varphi\\,d\\mu\\), integration over grids and the unit level interval yields\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathcal F}|\\nu_n^j\\varphi-\\alpha_n^j\\varphi|\n\\le{}&amp;\n2\\omega_\\varphi(\\sqrt2\\epsilon)\n+(B+1)\\|\\varphi\\|_\\infty\\alpha_n^j\\{|z|&gt;R\\}\\\\\n&amp;+\\|\\varphi\\|_\\infty\n\\left(\\sqrt{NV_v}+(B+1)Nn^{-c}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(7.5)\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(R,\\epsilon\\), let \\(n\\to\\infty\\), then \\(R\\to\\infty\\) and \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\). Equation (7.2) and \\(\\alpha_n^j\\Rightarrow\\beta_j\\) prove (7.4).</p><p>For incoming laws, restoring omitted targets changes the mean test discrepancy by at most \\(2\\|\\varphi\\|_\\infty\\) times their \\(o(1)\\) total \\(q_Y\\)-mass. Thus (7.4) holds in full \\(q_Y\\)-expectation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A subsequential weak limit \\(Q\\) exists.", "statement": "<p>Assume the bounded-\\(W\\) setting and conclusions of Lemmas 6.1 and 6.2. For a \\(q\\)-edge \\((x,y)\\) belonging to fiber \\(e\\), form the random object\n\\[\n\\bigl(\nU_1(x),U_2(x),U_1(y),U_2(y),\n(U_1)_*\\mu_e,(U_2)_*\\mu_e\n\\bigr)\n\\in S^4\\times\\mathcal P(S)^2.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A subsequential weak limit \\(Q\\) exists. Denote its four coordinate entries by \\((a,b,a',b')\\). Its last two entries equal \\((\\beta_1,\\beta_2)\\) almost surely. Moreover, \\(Q\\)-almost surely,\n\\[\na,b,a',b'\\in\\mathbb C,\\qquad\na\\ne a',\\quad b\\ne b',\\qquad\na'\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\beta_1,\\quad\nb'\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\beta_2.\n\\]\nThe coordinate \\(a\\) has law \\(\\beta_1\\) independently of the entire target pair \\((a',b')\\). Finally,\n\\[\n\\beta_2=\\Phi_*\\beta_1,\n\\qquad\n\\Phi(z)=b+\\frac{(a'-a)(b'-b)}{z-a}\n\\quad(z\\in S),\n\\tag{7.6}\n\\]\nalmost surely.</p>", "proof": "<p>The joint space is compact metrizable, so take a subsequential weak limit \\(Q\\).</p><p>To identify the measure entries simultaneously for all tests, choose a countable dense sequence \\((f_r)\\) in the unit ball of \\(C(S)\\). The metric\n\\[\nd(\\mu,\\beta)=\n\\sum_{r\\ge1}2^{-r}|\\mu f_r-\\beta f_r|\n\\]\ndefines weak convergence. Equation (7.4) for finitely many tests, followed by a bound on the series tail, gives convergence of this metric to zero in mean for each fiber projection. Passing to \\(Q\\) proves that the last entries equal \\((\\beta_1,\\beta_2)\\).</p><p>The inequality\n\\[\nq\\le B_0\\pi\\le2B_0\\alpha\\otimes\\alpha\n\\]\nimplies that each limiting same-coordinate pair law is dominated by \\(2B_0\\beta_j\\otimes\\beta_j\\). The inequality passes to the limit against nonnegative continuous tests on \\(S^2\\). Since \\(\\beta_j\\) is nonatomic and gives infinity mass zero, the stated finiteness, inequalities, and support memberships hold almost surely.</p><p>For \\(f\\in C(S)\\), \\(g\\in C(S^2)\\), conditioning on the actual target vertex gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\left|\n\\mathbb E_q f(U_1(x))g(U_1(y),U_2(y))\n-(\\beta_1f)\\mathbb E_{q_Y}g(U_1(y),U_2(y))\n\\right|\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad\\le\n\\|g\\|_\\infty\\,\n\\mathbb E_{q_Y}\n\\left|\n\\int f\\,d(U_1)_*\\rho_y-\\beta_1f\n\\right|\n\\longrightarrow0\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nby Lemma 6.2. Such products determine the joint measure on \\(S\\times S^2\\), proving that \\(a\\) has law \\(\\beta_1\\) independently of \\((a',b')\\). Both target coordinates are kept together in \\(g\\).</p><p>On the open domain of finite quadruples with \\(a\\ne a'\\), \\(b\\ne b'\\), put\n\\[\nt'=(a'-a)(b'-b).\n\\]\nFor each prelimit edge, its fiber projection measures satisfy the stated Möbius formula with the prelimit coordinates. Indeed, normalization multiplies the original fiber product by \\(e^{m_1+m_2}\\), and evaluation at \\(y\\) identifies the new product as\n\\[\n(U_1(y)-U_1(x))(U_2(y)-U_2(x)).\n\\]</p><p>The matrix\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nb&amp;t'-ab\\\\\n1&amp;-a\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nrepresents \\(\\Phi\\) on \\(S\\), with determinant \\(-t'\\ne0\\). The map is jointly continuous in its parameters and sphere variable, including at the pole. Its pushforward acts continuously on probability measures in the weak topology, by testing continuous functions and using uniform continuity on compact parameter neighborhoods.</p><p>Thus the equality graph is relatively closed over the stated open domain. Its complement within that domain is open in the ambient joint space and has zero mass in every prelimit law. Portmanteau gives it zero \\(Q\\)-mass. The domain has full \\(Q\\)-mass, so (7.6) follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "Lemma 7.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is no probability law on finite quadruples \\((a,b,a',b')\\) such that almost surely \\( a\\ne a',\\qquad b\\ne b',\\qquad a'\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\beta_1,\\qquad b'\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\beta_2, \\) such that \\(a\\) has …", "statement": "<p>Assumptions:\nLet \\(\\beta_1,\\beta_2\\) be nonatomic probability measures on \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is no probability law on finite quadruples \\((a,b,a',b')\\) such that almost surely\n\\[\na\\ne a',\\qquad b\\ne b',\\qquad\na'\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\beta_1,\\qquad\nb'\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\beta_2,\n\\]\nsuch that \\(a\\) has law \\(\\beta_1\\) independently of \\((a',b')\\), and such that, as measures on the Riemann sphere,\n\\[\n\\beta_2=\n\\left(z\\longmapsto\nb+\\frac{(a'-a)(b'-b)}{z-a}\n\\right)_*\\beta_1.\n\\]\nNo independence condition is imposed on \\(b\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a finite real random variable \\(X\\) and a continuous nonnegative function \\(w\\) supported in a compact subinterval of \\((0,1)\\), define\n\\[\nJ_w(X)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}w(\\Pr(X\\ge u))\\,du.\n\\]\nThis is finite: the tail tends to one at \\(-\\infty\\) and zero at \\(+\\infty\\), so the integrand vanishes outside a bounded interval. It is unchanged by adding a constant to \\(X\\).</p><p>Also,\n\\[\nX_k\\longrightarrow0\\text{ in probability}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nJ_w(X_k)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{7.7}\n\\]\nIndeed, choose \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) such that \\(w\\) vanishes outside \\([\\delta,1-\\delta]\\). For any \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), eventually\n\\[\n\\Pr(|X_k|\\ge\\epsilon)&lt;\\delta.\n\\]\nFor \\(u\\ge\\epsilon\\), the upper tail is below \\(\\delta\\); for \\(u\\le-\\epsilon\\), it is above \\(1-\\delta\\). Hence\n\\[\n0\\le J_w(X_k)\\le2\\epsilon\\|w\\|_\\infty,\n\\]\nproving (7.7).</p><p>Choose continuous weights \\(w_l:[0,1]\\to[0,1]\\), \\(l\\ge3\\), each supported inside \\((0,1)\\) and equal to one on \\([1/l,1-1/l]\\). Write \\(\\Phi\\) for the Möbius map in the statement. For \\(z\\ne a,a'\\),\n\\[\n\\Phi(z)-b'=(b'-b)\\frac{a'-z}{z-a},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n-\\log|\\Phi(z)-b'|\n=\n\\log\\frac{|z-a|}{|z-a'|}\n-\\log|b'-b|.\n\\]\nNonatomicity removes the exceptional points. The pushforward identity and translation invariance of \\(J_{w_l}\\) imply\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\int_{\\mathbb R}\nw_l\\!\\left(\n\\beta_1\\left\\{z:\n\\log\\frac{|z-a|}{|z-a'|}\\ge u\n\\right\\}\n\\right)\\,du\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad=\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}\nw_l\\!\\left(\n\\beta_2\\{z:-\\log|z-b'|\\ge u\\}\n\\right)\\,du.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(7.8)\n\\]\nThe random variables are finite almost surely, so the integrals are finite. They are measurable in their parameters: define the logarithmic kernels arbitrarily at coincidences, where the measures vanish, and apply measurability of parameter integrals to the resulting Borel functions.</p><p>Equality (7.8) depends only on \\((a,a',b')\\). Intersect the full-measure equality sets over all \\(l\\). Independence and Fubini imply that for almost every target pair \\((a',b')\\), all these equalities hold for \\(\\beta_1\\)-almost every \\(a\\). Fix such a target pair in the stated supports.</p><p>For \\(Z\\sim\\beta_2\\), the variable \\(-\\log|Z-b'|\\) is nonconstant. More explicitly, nonatomicity gives \\(r_0&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\np=\\beta_2\\{|z-b'|\\ge r_0\\}&gt;0,\n\\]\nand support membership gives \\(0&lt;r&lt;r_0\\) with\n\\[\nq=\\beta_2\\{|z-b'|&lt;r\\}&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor\n\\[\n-\\log r_0&lt;u&lt;-\\log r,\n\\]\nthe upper tail lies in \\([q,1-p]\\). Choose \\(l\\) with\n\\[\n1/l\\le\\min(p,q).\n\\]\nThe right side of (7.8) is strictly positive.</p><p>The full-\\(\\beta_1\\)-measure equality set contains a sequence \\(a_k\\ne a'\\) tending to \\(a'\\), since \\(a'\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\beta_1\\) and \\(\\beta_1\\{a'\\}=0\\). For \\(z\\) outside the null countable set \\(\\{a',a_1,a_2,\\ldots\\}\\),\n\\[\n\\log\\frac{|z-a_k|}{|z-a'|}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThese variables converge to zero in \\(\\beta_1\\)-probability. By (7.7), the left side of (7.8), for the fixed chosen \\(l\\), tends to zero. Its right side is a fixed positive number, a contradiction.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 0, "cite"], [2, 4, "cite"], [2, 7, "cite"], [2, 10, "cite"], [2, 11, "cite"], [2, 16, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [4, 0, "cite"], [4, 7, "cite"], [4, 10, "cite"], [4, 11, "cite"], [4, 16, "cite"], [5, 4, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 4, "cite"], [7, 0, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [7, 11, "cite"], [7, 16, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [9, 12, "cite"], [10, 0, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [10, 16, "cite"], [10, 17, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 11, "cite"], [15, 11, "cite"], [16, 0, "cite"], [17, 16, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [17, 19, "cite"], [18, 16, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [19, 16, "cite"], [20, 16, "cite"]]});
